# graphify knowledge graph — build output is per-developer and never committed
# (#1152). Untracked with `git rm --cached`, not just ignored: an ignore line
# alone cannot untrack an already-committed file. Build on demand with
# `scripts/graphify-local.sh build`; see docs/decisions/0007-untrack-the-graphify-artifact.md.
graphify-out/

# Eval run output — NOTHING under here is tracked on `main` (#1382, ADR 0015).
#
# #1333 (ADR 0012) deliberately narrowed this rule so the nightly domain-eval
# envelopes WERE tracked, because `drift-alert` reads them week-over-week. That
# reasoning is unchanged; what changed is where the history lives. #1308 decided
# this directory does not ship, #1382 implemented that as "untracked and
# gitignored", and the nightly now publishes its envelopes to the dedicated
# `eval-results` branch instead of `main` — the same orphan-branch pattern
# `cla.yml` already uses for `cla-signatures`. History still accumulates, still
# outlives a workflow-artifact retention window, and no longer grows `main` by
# ~365 files a year.
#
# The rule is the whole directory because nothing under it is tracked any more,
# so there is no negation to get wrong. `eval-domain-nightly.yml` restores the
# branch's contents into this directory before running, which is what keeps
# `drift-alert`'s baseline comparison working.
#
# `scripts/lib/eval-results-commit-guard-runner.test.mjs` asserts this shape
# against real git.
eval-results/

# Frozen documentation snapshots for the doc-retrieval eval corpora (#1382).
#
# These 38 files are byte-identical copies of this repository's own `docs/*.md`
# at `snapshotCommit` 953bfe7034cd7a4f7e3c5ca82b03642a0cdebcf7, so they are a
# second copy of content git already stores. Rebuild them from that commit with
# `pnpm eval:restore-corpus` — every file's sha256 and its `source` path are
# recorded in the corpus's committed `snapshot-manifest.json`, which is what
# makes the rebuild exact and verifiable rather than approximate.
#
# Everything ELSE under `eval-data/` stays tracked and is the irreplaceable
# half: `queries.json`'s span-anchored questions, `reference.json`'s
# hand-authored gold answers, the authoring guides, the manifests and both
# generator scripts. Only the regenerable snapshot leaves the index.
#
# `*` matches one path segment, so this targets `<corpus>/docs/` exactly and no
# parent directory is excluded — git can still see the sibling files it must
# keep tracking. A blanket `eval-data/` plus negations would NOT work: git
# refuses to re-include a path whose parent directory is excluded.
eval-data/corpus/*/docs/

# Local SAML test harness (#523) — the throwaway mock-IdP signing keypair is
# generated per-developer by scripts/saml-harness/up.sh. NEVER commit private
# keys (also trips the Semgrep secret-detection gate).
scripts/saml-harness/.keys/

# Claude Code — commit shared config (.claude/settings.json, .claude/agents/,
# .mcp.json) but keep each developer's machine-local files out of the repo.
.claude/settings.local.json
.claude/tmp/
CLAUDE.local.md

# Agent worktrees (#1147). Each is a full checkout plus its own `pnpm install`,
# so the tree is throwaway and large: one worktree measured 2.9 GB, of which
# essentially all is node_modules — the source checkout is ~4,100 files and
# cheap, and git worktrees share the object store, so neither the code nor
# `.git` is what costs. Nothing here has ever been committed (verified: zero
# commits touch the path), so unlike graphify-out/ (#1152) this needs no
# `git rm --cached` — the rule is purely to stop `git add -A` from staging
# several gigabytes of node_modules. Create and prune via the `worktree-hygiene`
# skill and `pnpm worktrees:prune`, never by hand-deleting the directory.
.claude/worktrees/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Default-deny for captures and environment files.
#
# The rules above name screenshot patterns one batch at a time, so a capture with
# a new name, or one level down, was committable. Screenshots of a running METIS
# show whatever project was loaded — treat every raster image, video and browser
# trace as sensitive. To ship one deliberately, review it and add a `!path` line.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
